package diag

// Explain holds the one-paragraph explanation printed by `alder explain <code>`
// for every diagnostic code the compiler raises.
var Explain = map[string]string{
	"E0001": "Parse error. The file does not follow Alder's grammar (chapter 3 and appendix A): a token was found where the parser expected something else, a string or element was left open, a declaration is malformed, an identifier contains two underscores in a row (`a__b`), or a module name segment ends with an underscore. Parsing reports every error it finds, then the compiler stops before name resolution so one typo does not produce a cascade. Fix the first error and re-run; later ones are often consequences.",
	"E0002": "Unknown name. An identifier, constructor, type, module, or message was used but nothing with that name is in scope (chapters 3 and 6). Check the spelling, make sure the name is declared, and remember that messages of the App module are written `App.Logout` and must be declared with `msg` in `App.alder`. For a name that exists in another module, import it (E0201) or qualify the type (E0202).",
	"E0003": "Type mismatch. An expression has one type where the context needs another (chapter 4). Alder has no implicit conversions except widening a brand to its base type and a `requires` page's `Session` to the app's `Session` (never the reverse), so `Int` in a `Text` position needs `Text.fromInt`, `+` does not join text (use `++`), and a `Time` is not a `Date`. The message names both types; convert the value or change the signature. Signatures of `load`, `view`, `update`, `error`, and `notFound` have fixed shapes (chapter 6), and `view` is always `Model -> Session -> Html Msg`. A `Dict` key may not be or contain a function or an effect value (`Query`, `Task`), also inside a union constructor's payload, whether the `Dict` type is written or only inferred (`Dict.insert inc \"a\" Dict.empty`): keys are compared with `==`, which functions and effects do not have; use a name or an id as the key. For the same reason `==` and `!=` reject functions and effect values: run an effect with `let x <-` and compare its result. A column method (`.eq`, `.gt`, `.asc`, …) needs its column written in place as `Table.field` (a column cannot be stored in a variable), and a `Db` function needs a concrete table: `Table Todo`, not `Table t`.",
	"E0004": "Non-exhaustive match. A `match` does not cover every constructor of the union it inspects (chapter 4). Every case must be handled so that adding a constructor later shows exactly where code needs updating. Add the missing arms listed in the message, or end with a `_` arm if a default is really intended.",
	"E0005": "Module path mismatch. A module's name must match its file path: `app/Todos/Index.alder` declares `module Todos.Index` (chapter 3). Rename the module line or move the file so the two agree.",
	"E0006": "Import cycle. Module A imports B which imports A, directly or through other modules (chapter 3). Modules form a directed acyclic graph. Break the cycle by moving the shared declarations into a third module that both import.",
	"E0007": "Missing type signature. Every top-level value needs a signature on the line before its definition (chapter 3). Signatures are the checked documentation of a module and the contract other modules rely on. Write `name : Type` above the definition; the fix inserts the type the compiler infers from the body, such as `rank : Priority -> Int`, or `name : ?type` as a placeholder to complete when the type cannot be fully inferred (a polymorphic value, or a body with another error).",
	"E0008": "Arity error. A function is defined, applied, or passed with the wrong number of parameters (chapter 3). The definition must name exactly as many parameters as its signature's arrows, a lambda must match the function type expected, and a constructor with no payload takes no argument. A type takes exactly as many type arguments as it declares parameters: `type Box a = …` is written `Box Int`, never `Box` or `Box Int Text`. Add or remove the argument.",
	"E0009": "Duplicate definition. Two declarations in the same scope share a name, or two imported modules declare a constructor with the same name (chapters 3 and 4). Constructors share one unqualified namespace across imports, so they must be unique. Rename one of them.",
	"E0010": "Missing required declaration. A module is missing something its role demands (chapter 6): a page module needs `load`, `view`, and `Model`, plus `update` if it declares any `msg`; `App` needs `error` and `notFound`, an `update` if it declares messages, and a `login` page and `session` if any page uses `requires`. Add the declaration named in the message.",
	"E0011": "Invalid declaration. The declaration is well-formed but breaks a rule of its kind (chapters 4, 6, and 8): a table needs exactly one `@id` field of a `brand Text` type, stored fields must be scalar, brands of scalars, or `Maybe` of those, page parameters and form fields must be decodable from URLs and forms, `requires` must name a `Maybe` session field, brand constructors may only be used in their own module or inside a `test`, and a record may not contain itself by value (`type R = { r: R }`, directly or through other records: no value of it could ever be built) — reach it through `Maybe`, `List`, `Dict` or a union instead. Change the declaration as the message describes.",
	"E0012": "Reserved name. The compiler generates some types in a page module and in App: `Params` (a page's parameters), `Msg` (the messages), `Session` (the session the module's code sees) and, for every `msg Pick`, the record `PickParams` of its parameters (chapter 6). A type, table, form or component of that module may not take one of these names, because it would be the same name as the generated type in the compiled program. Rename your declaration, for example `PickArgs` instead of `PickParams`.",
	"E0101": "`Db.get` takes the table's id type. `Db.get Post params.slug` fails because `slug` is not a `PostId`; `Db.get` looks a row up by primary key only (chapter 8). To look up by another column, query: `Db.from Post |> Db.where (Post.slug.eq slug) |> Db.first`, which returns a `Maybe Post`.",
	"E0102": "Field access on a `Maybe`. A value that may be absent, such as the result of `Db.get`, `Db.first`, or a `Maybe` session field, cannot be read with `.field` until it has been matched (chapters 4 and 8). Use `match value { Just v -> ..., Nothing -> ... }` and read the field from `v`; the fix inserts the skeleton.",
	"E0201": "Module not imported. A qualified name such as `Todo.Priority` or `Db.from Todo` refers to a module this file has not imported (chapter 3). Add `import Todo` after the `module` line. Standard-library modules such as `Db`, `List`, and `Text` are always available; pages and App messages are global.",
	"E0202": "Unqualified type from another module. A type declared in another module must be written with its module name, `Todo.Priority`, not `Priority` (chapters 3 and 4). Constructors, by contrast, are always unqualified. Qualify the type; the fix shows how.",
	"E0203": "Qualified constructor. Constructors are never qualified: write `Normal`, not `Todo.Normal` (chapter 4). Only types and values are qualified by their module. Remove the module prefix.",
	"E0301": "Message sent without its parameters. A `msg` declared with parameters, such as `msg Toggle { id: TodoId }`, must be sent with a record: `click={Toggle { id: todo.id }}` (chapter 6). A bare `Toggle` is a function, not a message. The fix provides the record with a `?hole` for each field.",
	"E0302": "`update` does not handle every message. The `update` of a page must have an arm for every `msg` the page declares (chapter 6), so a message can never be sent that nothing handles. Add the arms listed in the message; the fix inserts a skeleton.",
	"E0303": "`href` needs a page value. Links are checked: `href` takes a page applied to its parameters, such as `href={Todos.Index {}}`, or `Url.external \"https://...\"` for an outside address (chapter 5). A plain string could point at a route that does not exist.",
	"E0304": "Form input problem. `<Input>`, `<Textarea>`, `<Select>`, and `<Errors>` are bound to the form type of the message in the enclosing `<form submit={...}>` (chapters 5 and 6). `field` must be a literal that names a field of that form (the message must be declared with `form F`), and the component must suit the field's type: `<Select>` for unions, `<Input>` otherwise. A plain `<input>` inside such a form is an error. The message lists the form's fields.",
	"E0310": "Unreachable match arm. An arm can never run because earlier arms already handle every case it could match, for example a repeated constructor or a catch-all after full coverage (chapter 4). Delete the arm.",
	"E0401": "Write inside a read-only context. `load` and `Query` blocks can only read; `Db.insert`, `Db.update`, `Db.delete`, and other `Task` operations belong in `update` (chapters 7 and 8). This keeps page loads free of side effects. Move the write to `update` and return the result there.",
	"E0402": "`let x <-` outside an effect block. The bind arrow runs an effect and is only allowed in a block whose result is a `Query` or a `Task` (chapter 7). In a plain block use `let x = ...`; if you meant to run an effect, make the block's type `Query a` or `Task a`.",
	"E0501": "`Db.insert` never takes a generated column. The `@id`, `@created`, and `@updated` fields of a table are generated by the database layer, so the record given to `Db.insert` must leave them out (chapter 8). Remove the field from the record literal.",
	"E0502": "Wrong `@ref` type. A field declared `@ref Table` must have the id type of that table, or `Maybe` of it, such as `owner: User.UserId @ref User` (chapter 8). Using the table's other columns or a made-up id brand is an error. Change the field type to the referenced table's id.",
	"E0601": "`Msg` outside a page module. `Msg` is generated for each page module and for `App`; a shared module such as a layout or component library has no `Msg` of its own (chapters 5 and 6). Type shared components as `Html a`, so that the caller decides the message type. The fix replaces `Msg` with `a`.",
	"E0602": "Syntax from another language. The parser recognized a construct that Alder deliberately does not have (chapter 3): `case x of` (use `match x { ... }`), `let ... in` (use a block `{ let y = 1 ... }`), a tuple `(a, b)` (use a record `{ a: 1, b: 2 }`), or a type alias such as `type alias P = …` or `type Model = Unit` (a new name is always a new type: declare a record or a union with its own constructors). Rewrite it with the Alder form named in the message.",
	"W0001": "Unused import. The module is imported but none of its names are used in this file (chapter 3). The program is still valid; remove the `import` line to keep the module graph honest.",
	"W0002": "Typed hole. `?name` marks a place where an expression is still to be written (chapter 11). The program is checked with the hole present and the warning reports the type the hole must have, so a person or an agent can fill it in. Replace `?name` with an expression of that type.",
}
